#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:561
#: 5f2e0781388343f1a187453c8d1dbce1
msgid "Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:563
#: f42af8197ee64e0fb51096af71f59af5
msgid "Users gather critical information from these fields. With parameters that are well-documented in the code, the wiki and GCS can update parameters automatically. When parameters are properly documented, users can often tune their vehicles without needing pages and posts of external non-linked documentation. While information here is not a substitute for a tuning guide, it can be very effective at guiding users to change the right things."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:565
#: 00d00fc7c7bb4ccc8d7d477c0ffb4e6f
msgid "Parameter with multiple words should have the words ordered from left to right by importance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:567
#: 6a4eb38ffd6b458eb61e70a39da2f4f4
msgid "the flight mode, feature or sensor should be the first word.  I.e. a parameter relevant only to the RTL flight mode should start with \"RTL\" like \"RTL_ALT\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:568
#: e4a160d00a674179a4f35a37300027c9
msgid "qualifiers like \"MIN\", \"MAX\" or units (in the rare case they appear in the name) should be on the far right.  I.e RTL_ALT_MIN is better than RTL_MIN_ALT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:570
#: 4273859718b9496bb8fdaf28231a495b
msgid "Reuse words from other parameters if possible instead of creating new words.  For example we use \"MIN\" and \"MAX\" so these should be used instead of equivalent words like \"TOP\" and \"BOTTOM\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:572
#: b654b4722be2418c8a6f3b15f9a94ca4
msgid "Parameters should be in the standard unit (meters for distances, degrees for angles) but in cases where they are not the unit may (optionally) be appended to the end.  This is definitely not a requirement but is up to the developer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:574
#: 4ce079c438234d6694bc589e423ae44a
msgid "The total length of the parameter name must be 16 characters or less."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:578
#: ca4409ded53b4116abce0799d77d8fd2
msgid "Display Name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:580
#: b8330154517e4aa8acceb5201b90f4e1
msgid "The display name is typically a 2-5 word phrase that describes what the parameter changes. Often this is the Parameter Name spelled out in full words. Do not start with nondescriptive word like \"the.\" A good Display Name for PTCH_LIM_MAX_DEG is \"Maximum Pitch Angle\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:584
#: 014d82bf320b4076aca108b52eec4d49
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:586
#: 85004559684d4f7596943ca2cb70f73b
msgid "The description is a long text field for a complete description of the parameter and how changing it may affect vehicle behavior. It should be kept concise while giving the most critical information to the user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:601
#: d39cc384b0af43cb8bfa49c644dd578f
msgid "Avoid in Descriptions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:603
#: 7ca0ac66a48443a1a19cb9c5bba0f71e
msgid "Helping words and nondescriptive language such as \"This parameter changes..., you, etc.\" that is common to all parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:604
#: 4c26a9cb982f4694878a1e6a97b159c9
msgid "Referencing other parameters unless it is critical"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:605
#: cda253e77eaa4e888d73c11d18484432
msgid "Describing a 0 setting as \"disabled\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:606
#: 7cfdb68fbb8440cd979c64eef3ee90af
msgid "Default settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:608
#: 953dbe24b1d14c368079837e930d2d29
msgid "Encourage in Descriptions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:610
#: c0cad1dd5e1e4598a2b357914535c514
msgid "Present tense language"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:611
#: ea0ff6ae85984989af061c79bf2734e0
msgid "Consequences of changing the parameter (this also guides users how to tune for their vehicle)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:612
#: 368c75b7cca5406c8e7d91df8ef942ec
msgid "When the parameter is used or ignored"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:613
#: 32e929536f6a4871b56f3526ef9de241
msgid "When a 0 setting uses another parameter for the value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:617
#: 02917401a05a488f87e47e78a19e1eb8
msgid "Value, Unit, Range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:619
#: 0840307093d74b8abbd3d957e1809569
msgid "The values, units, ranges, and steps are all critical for adjusting the parameter as well. Include them when possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:623
#: 4338b66965c34beb9fd5504db9460907
msgid "User"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:625
#: 8dd65a8be8704f17a4e0b347ce3595ff
msgid "The user field helps to categorize and hide advanced parameters from being adjusted by new users. There are currently 2 user fields:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:627
#: 416e90183d104ad493537f5855b2fd82
msgid "Standard - Available to anyone"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:628
#: 537158c325ef4cbdb19cdd03da6bfd4e
msgid "Advanced - Available to advanced users"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:631
#: e539a8915dfb4edb8e4bbfecaabe13b3
msgid "Floating Point Annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:633
#: 844088b765fe4ada98a4e6d6dbee23e0
msgid "ArduPilot is compiled with ``-fsingle-precision-constant``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:635
#: 4867a08d74e240a4a0254d126514b7a4
msgid "That means it is currently allowable to leave off the float specifier from constants.  It is also permissible to have them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:637
#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:649
#: cd5f88df8a8942819884c73c1625a9d7
#: aff4e21048244de2988d79faa6b8ed7d
msgid "**Right**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:645
#: c70ceccdcbc84889b7b5c03b785fd379
msgid "Multiplication vs Division"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:647
#: da180c7ffdee4bef8e677a43cf3355b0
msgid "Use multiplication rather than division where possible:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:655
#: 70c57e7c57ee4935b8a5aca0acfafd6f
msgid "**Wrong**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:661
#: e2651121a5bf4890b7c7f1d3b56a042d
msgid "Multiplications typically take fewer cycles to compute than divisions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:665
#: 14bfc7c6d6234aba90c9a82d75103277
msgid "ArduPilot Design Decisions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:667
#: be50736c9f03463385ae5683aa4cd37c
msgid "Several design decisions have been in the ArduPilot codebase to accommodate its embedded nature which may surprise some programmers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:670
#: 6827fcff9fb14066a44d69b5757e95d6
msgid "Implicit Zeroing of Memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:672
#: 04ca664ba36a4ac3821206a9cc946a27
msgid "Implicitly zeroing of memory gives us more consistent (even-if-bad) behaviour, and saves us flash space as most places in the code don't need to initialise the memory they've allocated.  The only memory you MUST zero is stack-stored variables - locals, asprintf and the like."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:674
#: 815b24c51e8b416d85c3d22f365e83be
msgid "new and malloc both zero their memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:675
#: 5824e8c2a62644a787e23ceaef9105c8
msgid "bss-stored data does not need to be zeroed (so no members in a singleton object need to be zeroed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:676
#: 6308fd8a3e01403db45d1a8871c82819
msgid "Vectors are special and zero themelves - even on the stack"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:677
#: c2f719b39dad4f8b9eabbd6f7926bd97
msgid "static variables within a function (which we generally frown upon) do not need to be zeroed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:681
#: 9c66f96c90c34a158eee1b62e1870e5b
msgid "Bit fields are generally frowned upon"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:683
#: 2427b335325444aeb84fedec80ee763d
msgid "Using bit fields reduces RAM usage but can considerably increase flash usage, as to extract a boolean truth value from a bit field requires more machine instructions.  If the variable is frequently accessed then this can be a LOT of flash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:685
#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:711
#: 60c1f3330cf1402eb5353201f9cf9dbe
#: 35226f79f0214eda8734fa6067afe5e1
msgid "**Not preferred:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:695
#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:722
#: fc84b2b42d144012874e75fbf7dc3ec3
#: 515fd8e263e24191953ed5f1b63bc0c3
msgid "**Preferred:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:707
#: 00b258832e094a0eb3ae5ddcda92ba65
msgid "Initialise member variables in header files rather than in constructors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:709
#: 6c52613ac70c4125ae30529d81bab8c4
msgid "Where a member isn't dependent on a constructor parameter, we prefer to do in-class-definition initialisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:736
#: f58ba4adadac4309986a35932a28de89
msgid "No Standard Library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:738
#: f4aa2d48267941b8bfc144414a2ce64b
msgid "For efficiency reasons, ArduPilot doesn't use the C standard library (``std::``).  We also prefer to use functions which are consistent across platforms to ease support (not using 64-bit maths on platforms that support it is useful, for example)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:740
#: 5ef663cf872c48dca76ab8424f244c84
msgid "This means no ``std::vector``, no ``std::string`` and no ``std::unordered_map``, for example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:742
#: 46ec283348a54e12982fc73f1d6e0ae8
msgid "We try to avoid library calls that handle their own allocations - but if you really want to go that way, including the header to get these isn't sufficient - you will need to fiddle with the build system to link ``std`` in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:745
#: f026d7e383144d2091886b2e615b508d
msgid "Alternatives to ``std::vector``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:747
#: 5bacf577482146bba062604b0445c977
msgid "Most of the time fixed-length arrays are used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:749
#: b158746679e5406e8ea69d9ed0396347
msgid "``AP_ExpandingArray`` may be an option for you - but expanding in-flight might be a bad thing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:751
#: d51fcf9d084b42f7bf146c236cc2843b
msgid "Linked lists are used on some places."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:755
#: 8b36539ecd33440e927d42527bc2a7ca
msgid "Alternatives to ``std::string``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:757
#: 832de280453c4b61ba8e6c250cf3bd70
msgid "``asprintf`` is used in some places.  Generally simply using ``char*`` is adequate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:760
#: 581b50297be642f48bedc555d2cc6d3a
msgid "Alternatives to ``std::unordered_map``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:762
#: c3479796b5ed424989a9d9df4fd04508
msgid "Create an array of structs and iterate it.  This is done when turning a mavlink id into an ap_message id, for example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:764
#: 416dc05e4c2542abac0a7a4026df30e9
msgid "Create an ordered array of structs and bisect-search it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:766
#: b6175c5bd4b44052ae7984292f3ef3f8
msgid "Create an array of structs with a perfect hash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:769
#: da3a248ec6184aa4bd1000751d6b6fa2
msgid "No Dead Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/style-guide.rst:771
#: 33dc723c03684f22bc9f82515cdf7d48
msgid "We don't keep dead code in ArduPilot.  If code is unused, it should be removed - not just commented out.  This is a general rule and not universally adhered to."
msgstr ""
